# isopod runtime tiers — how `isopod doctor` classifies the configured runtime
# (the hardening.conf `runtime` directive / ISOPOD_RUNTIME), whether `isopod
# create` sizes a default --memory for it, and which runtime it auto-selects.
#
#   tier 2  syscall sandbox, shared host kernel, no /dev/kvm needed (e.g. gVisor)
#   tier 3  microVM, separate guest kernel, needs /dev/kvm (e.g. Kata, krun)
#
# Columns: <runtime-name> <tier> <net>. <net> is the guest network stack, which
# decides whether SSH TCP-forwarding and bulk transfers work — VSCodium
# Remote-SSH (`isopod code`) and `isopod copy`/`export` all depend on both:
#   virtio    real virtio-net interface — forwarding and bulk transfers work
#   netstack  gVisor user-space netstack (Tier 2) — works
#   tsi       libkrun Transparent Socket Impersonation — SSH exec channels are
#             fast, but bulk data over an SSH TCP port-forward into a guest
#             loopback service STALLS. That is exactly how VSCodium/Cursor/
#             JetBrains reach their remote server, so `isopod code` (Remote-SSH)
#             can't connect. isopod's own shell/copy/export/fetch use exec
#             channels and are unaffected. isopod never auto-selects a `tsi`
#             runtime as the default microVM; it stays usable on explicit request
#             (--runtime / ISOPOD_RUNTIME) for those shell/data workflows.
#             Upstream libkrun bugs: containers/libkrun#579 (TSI drops large
#             transfers — BufDescTooSmall) + #510 (guest loopback wrongly proxied).
#
# The name matches what you pass to --runtime (and the `runtime` directive /
# ISOPOD_RUNTIME). Unlisted runtimes still work; doctor just cannot name their
# tier and create skips the memory default.
runsc         2  netstack
runsc-kvm     2  netstack
kata          3  virtio
kata-runtime  3  virtio
kata-qemu     3  virtio
kata-fc       3  virtio
kata-clh      3  virtio
krun          3  tsi

# NOT listed: crun-vm. It runs VM DISK images (a "containerdisk", a --rootfs disk
# image, or a bootc bootable container), not a plain OCI container image — which
# is what isopod builds and hands the runtime. It cannot boot an isopod box, so it
# is neither auto-selected nor suggested. kata is the supported Tier 3 microVM.
